# 1. Hub (Layer 1 – Physical Layer)
   A Hub is a basic, non-intelligent device used to connect multiple computers in a LAN. Think of it as a "signal repeater."

🛠️ How it works:
It receives a signal on one port and forwards it to all other ports, regardless of destination.
Has no understanding of MAC or IP addresses.
Causes collision domains (data clashing).


📌 Types of Hubs:
Passive Hub: Simply connects wires, no power.
Active Hub: Amplifies signals.
Intelligent Hub: Some diagnostics features.

🚫 Limitations:
Very inefficient.
No traffic control.
Replaced by switches.

✅ Use Case (now obsolete):
Used in early Ethernet networks (10 Mbps or 100 Mbps).

# 2. Switch (Layer 2 – Data Link Layer)
📘 What it is:
   A Switch is a smart device that connects computers in a LAN and forwards data to the correct device based on MAC address.

🛠️ How it works:
Switch maintains a MAC Address Table.
When it receives a frame, it checks destination MAC and sends only to that port (unicast).
Reduces collisions significantly.

📌 Advanced Features:
VLAN support: Isolate traffic within networks.
Port Mirroring: For network monitoring.

Layer 3 Switch: Also performs routing (works on IP addresses).

⚡ Use Case:
Backbone of modern LAN networks.

# 3. Bridge (Layer 2)
📘 What it is:
   A Bridge connects two LAN segments and filters traffic to reduce congestion.

🛠️ How it works:
Stores a table of MAC addresses per segment.
Only forwards data if destination is on the other segment.
Helps in segmentation.

🆚 Difference from Switch:
Switch: Multi-port and faster.
Bridge: Two-port and software-based.

✅ Use Case:
Used in older LAN setups or for protocol bridging.

# 4. Router (Layer 3 – Network Layer)
📘 What it is:
A Router connects multiple networks, usually a LAN to the Internet, and routes data based on IP addresses.

🛠️ How it works:
Maintains a Routing Table (Static or Dynamic).
Makes decisions using routing protocols (RIP, OSPF, BGP).
Uses NAT to translate private IP to public IP.
Assigns IP addresses (via DHCP).

📌 Types of Routers:
Wired/Wireless Router
Core Router (used by ISPs)
Edge Router (connects to external networks)

⚡ Use Case:
In every home, office, data center for connecting networks.

# 5. Gateway (All Layers – Mostly Layer 7)
📘 What it is:
A Gateway acts as a protocol translator between networks using different protocols (e.g., TCP/IP ↔ AppleTalk).

🛠️ How it works:
Converts data formats, header information, and sometimes encryption.

Operates across all layers of OSI (mainly application layer).

Often combines with router/firewall functionality.

⚡ Use Case:
IoT platforms (CoAP ↔ HTTP)

Email gateways (SMTP ↔ X.400)

VoIP calls (SIP ↔ PSTN)

🔹 6. Modem (Layer 1 – Physical Layer)
📘 What it is:
Modem = MOdulator + DEModulator.
It converts digital signals to analog and vice versa for telephone/cable transmission.

🛠️ How it works:
Your computer sends digital data.
Modem converts it to analog for transmission over copper lines.
Receiver modem converts it back to digital.

📌 Types:
DSL Modem
Cable Modem
Fiber Modem (ONT)

⚡ Use Case:
Used in home networks, especially DSL or cable internet.

🔹 7. Access Point (Layer 2)
📘 What it is:
An Access Point (AP) allows wireless devices (laptops, phones) to connect to a wired network using Wi-Fi.

🛠️ How it works:
Connects to a router/switch via Ethernet.
Transmits Wi-Fi signals (SSID).
Handles MAC authentication, sometimes DHCP relay.

📌 Types:
    Standalone AP: Basic home usage.

    Controller-based AP: Used in enterprise for central management.

⚡ Use Case:
    Offices, homes, airports, campuses.

 